      "threat_level": "dangerous"
    },
    {
      "name": "login",
      "description": "Sign in to Notion. Opens Notion's sign-in page in the browser (the link is also printed) and waits until the user approves which pages and databases to share. Blocks until approved, declined or timed out, so dispatch it as a concurrent task with a reminder, and tell the user about the link when it shows up in the output. Returns which workspace was connected; tokens are never returned.",
      "threat_level": "elevated",
      "blocking": true,
      "suggestedRemindAfter": 180
    },
    {
      "name": "status",
      "description": "Report whether JARVIS is signed in to Notion, and to which workspace. Makes no network call.",
      "threat_level": "safe"
    },
    {
      "name": "logout",
      "description": "Forget the stored Notion sign-in on this machine. To remove access entirely, disconnect JARVIS in Notion's settings.",
      "threat_level": "elevated"
    }
  ],
  "withheldTools": {
    "Bulk deletion: the forbidden tier.": [
      "notion-delete-databases",
      "notion-delete-pages"
    ],
    "Their descriptions and results steer the model toward upgrade links.": [
      "notion-check-mcp-next-steps",
      "notion-get-tool-access",
      "notion-show-advanced-analysis-next-steps"
    ],
    "Needs Notion AI and reaches connected sources (Slack, mail). Its description, like notion-search's, routes the model through notion-get-tool-access, which is withheld; notion-search covers Notion content. Notion has listed it on some days and not others.": [
      "notion-ai-search"
    ]
  },
  "trust": {
    "reviewReferences": [
      "https://developers.notion.com/docs/mcp",
      "https://developers.notion.com/docs/mcp-supported-tools",
      "https://github.com/makenotion/notion-mcp-server"
    ],
    "checks": [
      "wrapper: first-party stdlib Python in this repository; shared helper (oauthbrowser, httpproxy, hostpin, tokenstore, browser) vendored from servers/_lib, identical copy enforced by the PR gate",
      "upstream: Notion's first-party hosted MCP server at mcp.notion.com; nothing of Notion's runs locally",
      "auth: MCP authorization spec, checked live 2026-09-30: protected-resource metadata names mcp.notion.com as authorization server; dynamic client registration; PKCE S256; public clients; the wrapper registers itself, so no app registration is needed",
      "pinning: discovery, registration, token exchange, renewal and every forwarded call are pinned to mcp.notion.com; the redirect lands on a one-shot 127.0.0.1 listener that ignores requests without the sign-in's state",
      "tokens: kept in the OS keyring (secret-tool) or an owner-only file, renewed before expiry or after a 401, never returned in a tool result or logged",
      "access: the integration only sees the pages and databases the user shares with it during sign-in",
      "tools: 58 declared: Notion's 36 documented tools, the 19 more its live tools/list exposes (2 withheld deletes excluded), and the wrapper's login/status/logout; checked against the live server 2026-10-14"
    ],
    "notes": "Supersedes the hosted-transport draft (#105): the sign-in lives in this wrapper, not in dmcp (Project-JARVIS#229 v3). The tool list was checked against Notion's live tools/list on 2026-10-14: the live server exposes 55 tools, 21 of them beyond its documentation. 19 of those are declared here; notion-delete-pages and notion-delete-databases are withheld by the wrapper (mass moves to Trash, the forbidden tier's class). notion-ai-search and notion-list-agents are documented but were not live on the tested plan. A live tool missing here is still gated at elevated by JARVIS's registry-tier floor.",
    "securityNotes": "Reads are elevated because page content, comments and ai-search results can carry text anyone with edit access wrote (prompt-injection surface). Page edits are elevated rather than dangerous because Notion keeps page history. Schema changes and Custom Agent sessions are dangerous: the first can drop a property's data across a whole database, the second hands an autonomous agent the workspace and its connected tools."
  },
